\subsection{Response encodings and residual definitions}{

Factor-valued binomial responses are Bernoulli observations, even when
likelihood weights exceed one. Numeric proportions use the fitted trial
counts; paired success/failure counts retain their grouped-binomial encoding.
Default response simulations and CDFs follow that distinction. Explicit GAM
sampling weights can change trial counts or response precision, including
Gaussian AR1 precision, without changing fitted coefficient uncertainty.
MASS negative-binomial GLM families use their fitted size parameter.
The native gaulss likelihood ignores fitting weights, so its default
diagnostic references use the fitted inverse standard deviation directly.
Explicit sampling precision weights remain available; see the sampling
documentation for how these change the response distribution.

Native beta-regression simulation clips draws at epsilon and one minus
epsilon. These endpoints therefore have probability mass. Diagnostics include
that mass in their CDFs and randomisation and use moments of the same clipped
distribution. This is labelled an approximation: the fitting likelihood
evaluates the underlying beta density at epsilon-adjusted observations.
Distribution functions supplied by callers are preserved.

GEV and sinh-arcsinh checks use stable response means and correct residual
centering and signs for both observed and simulated responses. Their deviance
magnitudes follow the fitting package's definitions: GEV uses the square root
of its saturated log-likelihood contrast; sinh-arcsinh uses zero log density
as its reference. If a sinh-arcsinh density exceeds one, that contrast is
negative. Deviance checks then give an error rather than replace the residual
with zero; response and PIT/quantile checks remain available. These deviance
definitions do not produce comparable standardised scores across families.
Use PIT/quantile residuals for uniform/standard-normal references.

GEV means and variances require shape below one and one-half, respectively.
Signed deviance checks also require a finite mean and shape at least minus
one. PIT/quantile checks do not require these moments. When the response mean
does not exist, appraisal explains why the observed-versus-fitted panel is
unavailable and retains the PIT/quantile panels.

nlme calculations retain covariance between random effects, including nested
groups and block designs. They also retain the contrasts and observation order
used for fitting, including after subsetting or missing-value removal. Changing
global contrast options after fitting does not change the diagnostics.
}

\subsection{Conditioning and dependence}{

For response references, \code{conditioning = "conditional"} usually holds fitted
random effects and nuisance parameters fixed. \code{"marginal"} simulates new
effects from their fitted distributions and averages over them. Setting random
effects to zero is not a substitute for this averaging, especially with
nonlinear response links. sdmTMB has additional field choices described below.
PPCs separately include approximate uncertainty in fitted parameters.

\code{"gam_component"} explicitly uses the extracted GAM component. gamm and
gamm4 diagnostics and posterior predictive checks default to the full model
with conditional conditioning. gamm4 response simulations use the same default;
the existing gamm response simulator retains its GAM-component default.
Conditional response references hold fitted effects fixed, while conditional
PPCs include uncertainty in those fitted effects. Specify \code{"marginal"} to
simulate new effects instead.

Conditional glmmTMB/lme4 response likelihoods factorize given all random
effects, including temporal and spatial effects. Marginal responses may be
dependent because they share those effects. An independent effect for each
observation need not create dependence between observations. The calculations
check covariance implied by the fitted random-effect design, including
correlated slopes and covariance cancellation. Gaussian nlme residual
correlation can remain after conditioning on random effects; variance weights
alone do not introduce correlation.

A conditional check at estimated effects assesses the response distribution
at those estimates. It does not provide a calibrated test of random-effect
covariance or of the whole fitted model. Marginal joint references assess
response variation implied by new simulated effects. A QQ plot checks the
distribution of residuals; inspect their ordering or spatial pattern as well
to assess dependence. Models are not refitted when calculating reference bands.
}

\subsection{BAM working correlation}{

Gaussian identity-link AR1 BAM retains joint response simulation, with new
independent series starting at \code{AR.start} boundaries. For non-Gaussian or
nonidentity models fitted with \code{discrete = TRUE} and nonzero \code{rho}, BAM applies
AR1 to working residuals as a generalized estimating equations (GEE)
approximation. This does not specify a unique joint response distribution.
The restriction applies to supported \code{extended.family} and GLM families.
See \url{https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/mgcv/html/bam.html}.

Fitted marginal CDFs, inverse CDFs and PIT/quantile residuals remain useful.
For traditional residuals, the default QQ plot and a requested inverse-CDF
QQ plot use \code{method = "marginal"}. This approximates the pooled distribution
of individual residuals by numerical sampling, without asserting independence
or supplying dependence bands. Explicit \code{method = "simulate"}, joint response
simulation and PPCs give an explanatory error. No additional dependence model,
such as a copula, is introduced. An explicitly requested \code{"normal"} approximation
is available without independence-based bands; it is never the default here.

\code{\link[=residual_correlation]{residual_correlation()}} and \code{\link[=residual_correlation_plot]{residual_correlation_plot()}} provide approximate
checks of correlation between residuals at different lags. For non-Gaussian
BAM, the stored \code{std.rsd} residuals differ from the weighted working residuals
used in fitting. Adjusting working residuals for the fitted AR1 correlation
(whitening) or using \code{std.rsd} can reveal remaining serial structure. Neither
supplies a generative response model or calibrated p-values. Series boundaries
and gaps from missing observations are retained, and no significance bands
based on independence are drawn. Lags count observation positions, not spatial
distance or irregular elapsed time. Specify series and order the fitting data
to suit the dependence being checked.
}

\subsection{sdmTMB field choices}{

With fixed-effect coefficients and covariance parameters held at their
maximum likelihood estimates (MLEs):
\itemize{
\item \code{field_method = "mle-mvn"} (default) follows sdmTMB's recommended approach:
draw the fitted fields together from their approximate posterior distribution,
then hold that draw fixed for conditional PITs and response references.
Response errors are independent given those fields. Reference bands use
the same draw as the observed residuals.
\item \code{"mle-eb"} holds empirical-Bayes field estimates fixed. This matches sdmTMB's
residual construction at fitted fields, which its documentation does not
recommend for goodness-of-fit testing. Shrinkage and estimation can distort
the resulting residual distribution.
\item \code{"mle-mvn-integrated"} averages over approximate uncertainty in fitted fields
using sdmTMB's joint response simulations. Residuals are calculated from ranks
in these simulations rather than from one fixed draw of fields. This reuses
the fitting data and can give conservative checks; treat the results as
approximate posterior predictive checks.
\item \code{conditioning = "marginal"} instead simulates new spatial, spatiotemporal
and grouping effects. Simulation at fitting observations also renews other
supported field and time-varying effects; smooths remain fitted. \code{field_method}
does not cause another field draw in this mode.
}

\code{gratia.diagnostics.field_seed} (default 1) makes the posterior field draw
reproducible independently of PIT randomisation. All panels use the same draw.
To inspect further draws, change this option and present the results together.
The sdmTMB definitions and recommendations are documented at
\url{https://sdmtmb.github.io/sdmTMB/reference/residuals.sdmTMB.html} and
\url{https://sdmtmb.github.io/sdmTMB/articles/residual-checking.html}.

PPCs use a separate approximation that draws fixed-effect, nuisance and field
parameters together. Marginal PPCs simulate new fields at each parameter draw;
smooths retain their posterior draws. Plot subtitles identify these choices.
Parameter estimation, the Gaussian approximation to the posterior and finite
simulation error affect these checks. None of these modes guarantees that
goodness-of-fit tests attain their nominal false-positive rate after fitting.
}

\subsection{Calculating reference values and bands}{

Analytic PIT/quantile QQ plots use uniform/standard-normal reference quantiles.
For ideal independent responses, bands are pointwise intervals for ordered
residuals. For dependent responses, bands come from joint simulations
(\code{n_simulate}) when a joint generator exists. Otherwise the bands are omitted
and the plot explains why. Bands condition on the chosen fitted distribution
and fields. They do not provide simultaneous coverage or account for refitting.

When a joint simulator is available but marginal CDFs or moments cannot be
calculated analytically, one reference sample of simulated data sets supplies
means, variances and residual ranks. With \code{R} reference draws, PIT is
\verb{(number below + U * (ties + 1))/(R + 1)}, where \code{U} is uniform on \verb{(0, 1)}.
The extra rank position is for the observation being checked, which keeps
normal scores finite even outside the simulated range. Observed and simulated
responses use the same reference sample, centering and residual definitions.
These ranks need not equal the empirical CDF at an observation.

\code{options(gratia.diagnostics.marginal_draws = 249L)} sets the reference sample
size (minimum 19). Increase it to assess sensitivity to Monte Carlo error.
\code{gratia.diagnostics.marginal_seed} (default 1) makes this sample reproducible
independently of residual randomisation. A finite sample limits tail resolution
and moment accuracy. Moment estimates require finite population moments:
glmmTMB Student-t response residuals require df > 1, and Pearson residuals
require df > 2. An undefined moment is not replaced by a finite sample estimate.
PIT/quantile checks and joint draws remain available, but an undefined mean
cannot be shown as a fitted expectation. Bands for residuals calculated from
simulation ranks use joint draws and the same rank transformation, even if
the underlying responses are independent. Marginal deviance residuals remain
unavailable where the integrated likelihood has no defined deviance; use
response, Pearson or quantile residuals instead.
}

\subsection{Remaining limitations and extensions}{

Inverse CDFs with direct lower/upper and log-probability arguments preserve
extreme interior quantiles. Older functions that accept only lower-tail
probabilities cannot always represent an extreme upper-tail probability.
If conversion rounds it to an endpoint and produces a false infinite quantile,
an explanatory error is raised. This limits extreme upper quantiles of the
compound-Poisson Tweedie distribution. The Gamma boundary and inverse-Gaussian
solver accept log probabilities directly. True probability endpoints remain valid.

BAM/PQL working correlations need a scientifically specified joint response
model before joint simulation or PPC can be added. Additional GJRM and other
fitting-package families should supply their parameters to the shared response
distributions. Censored/survival PPCs need a way to reapply censoring to each
simulated response. Nominal multivariate PITs need a documented category
ordering or a different multivariate diagnostic. Unrecognised random-effect
structures remain unsupported. Diagnostics do not repair \code{na.pass} fits where
missing responses cannot be aligned with predictions and residuals; ordinary
\code{na.omit}/\code{na.exclude} fits are covered. MCMC, model refitting and calibrated
dependence tests would require additional sampling or refitting methods.
}

\subsection{Large data sets and family functions}{

Like mgcViz, these diagnostics use residuals with known reference distributions
where available and aggregate large data sets before plotting. All observations
and QQ endpoints contribute. Kernel density estimates and histograms remain
available as separate views. See
\url{https://mfasiolo.github.io/mgcViz/reference/qq.gamViz.html}.
mgcViz approximates QQ plots on a discrete grid; gratia aggregates plot points
but preserves exact pooled reference quantiles. mgcViz also plots differences
between empirical and model-implied conditional densities. Adding such plots
would require comparable smoothing bandwidths and a validated conditional
reference. The existing density and histogram views retain information about
scale misspecification rather than rescaling every residual to unit variance.
See \url{https://mfasiolo.github.io/mgcViz/reference/l_densCheck.html}.

Uniform references average ordered residuals as draws are generated, without
storing all draws. Simulated QQ uses exact pooled quantiles computed as by
\code{stats::quantile(type = 7)} and pointwise intervals for each residual rank.
Above \code{options(gratia.diagnostics.max_cells = 1e7)}, sorted draws are written
to temporary files and merged exactly. This limits simulation memory use at
the cost of disk I/O. One response vector and the reference output must still
fit in memory. Scalable PPCs summarise every draw on the same grid; discrete
responses with more than 512 distinct values use labelled probabilities for
512 bins. \code{\link[=posterior_samples]{posterior_samples()}} returns the raw response draws. Coefficient
and random-effect draws still need memory proportional to the number of
parameters times the number of draws.

The internal \code{fix_family_rd()}, \code{fix_family_cdf()} and \code{fix_family_qf()}
functions still add \verb{$rd}, \verb{$cdf} and \verb{$qf} to mgcv-compatible families while
preserving functions supplied by callers. The fitted-distribution code adapts
their arguments locally. Other fitting packages can use the same standard
response distributions, or combine them by family index, truncate them or add
point masses without changing the plotting calculations.
}
